       :ast (app (v 'list) (lit (list 1 2.5))),\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:543:                                      [:fingerprint :records])]\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:572:(deftest list-literals-persist-in-memory\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:573:  ;; the durable file codec does not carry lists on every host; the\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:574:  ;; pipeline itself does, and keeps a list distinct from a vector\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:576:        as-list (persist! (app (v 'list) (lit (list 1 2.5))) (recording-writer) store)\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:577:        as-vector (persist! (app (v 'list) (lit [1 2.5])) (recording-writer) store)]\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:578:    (is (= :ok (:outcome (:projected as-list)) (:outcome (:projected as-vector))))\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:579:    (is (= {:yin.debruijn/fingerprint (get-in as-list [:projected :fingerprint]),\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:583:                                               (app (v 'list) (lit (list 1 2.5)))))))}\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:584:           (jing/get store (get-in as-list [:projected :address]) ::absent)))\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:585:    (is (not= (get-in as-list [:projected :fingerprint])\ntest/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:586:              (get-in as-vector [:projected :fingerprint])))))\ndocs/design/datom.md:2:description: Moduli space of tuples; meta-protocol over open dimensions; N-dimensional tuples, entity IDs, content addressing, namespaces; datom defined as [e a v t m]; source scope is interpreter context, never a tuple slot\ndocs/design/datom.md:7:Tuples are elements in a moduli space, graded by dimension n.\ndocs/design/datom.md:8:A tuple can be any dimension/size; each dimension dn is a distinct kind of fact, fit for a distinct role.\ndocs/design/datom.md:9:A datom is the canonical persistent tuple: [e a v t m].\ndocs/design/datom.md:10:The moduli space is open: applications declare new dimensions as needed. No dimension is canonical.\ndocs/design/datom.md:20:  d3: (s, a, v). Bare facts (RDF-style triples). The semantic floor for fact-shaped dimensions.\ndocs/design/datom.md:25:  d1 (content-addressing floor): every tuple of any dimension reduces to its hash, itself a d1 tuple. Universal addressability.\ndocs/design/datom.md:26:  d3 (semantic floor): every fact-shaped dimension projects to (subject, attribute, value). Universal interpretability for the fact-shaped subset.\ndocs/design/datom.md:28:Not every dimension is fact-shaped. Some are tuple-shaped without subject (capabilities, signatures, sensor frames).\ndocs/design/datom.md:31:Universal principles (apply to all dimensions):\ndocs/design/datom.md:36:  Restrictions are a feature: dimension choice constrains shape, enabling efficient per-shape indexing.\ndocs/design/datom.md:40:  Each dimension is a different chart on that universe.\ndocs/design/datom.md:41:  A tuple at dimension n is a tuple-shaped event in n coordinates.\ndocs/design/datom.md:42:  A datom (the canonical d5 5-tuple [e a v t m]) is like a space-time event:\ndocs/design/datom.md:45:  Interpreters observe parts of the stream and construct higher-dimensional structures.\ndocs/design/datom.md:47:  Higher-dimensional tuples can serve specialized streams, for example\ndocs/design/datom.md:53:The meta-protocol is the only universal piece. The vocabulary of dimensions is open.\ndocs/design/datom.md:55:A dimension dn is declared as a small d3 subgraph:\ndocs/design/datom.md:56:  :dim/arity         n (the slot count).\ndocs/design/datom.md:57:  :dim/slots         ordered list of (slot-name, slot-type, slot-role).\ndocs/design/datom.md:58:  :dim/encoding      canonical-encoding rule (parameterized by slot types).\ndocs/design/datom.md:59:  :dim/projection-to declared morphism dn -> dm (m < n).\ndocs/design/datom.md:60:  :dim/lift-from     declared morphism dm -> dn (with default-supplying function or pointer).\ndocs/design/datom.md:62:The content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity.\ndocs/design/datom.md:63:New dimensions are introduced by publishing such a bundle. No external schema authority is needed.\ndocs/design/datom.md:66:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\ndocs/design/datom.md:67:  Different dimensions never collide on the same slot values.\ndocs/design/datom.md:73:  Each new dn publishes morphisms to/from anchor dimensions (typically d3 for fact-shaped, d1 universally).\ndocs/design/datom.md:77:  Two tuples are equal iff their canonical hashes match within the same dimension.\ndocs/design/datom.md:78:  Cross-dimensional equality goes through projection: a in dn matches b in dm iff pi(a) = b.\ndocs/design/datom.md:82:Every slot type has a canonical encoding rule that all implementations must conform to.\ndocs/design/datom.md:87:  Floats:   IEEE 754; NaN normalized; +0 and -0 distinct or unified per declaration; denormals as-is.\ndocs/design/datom.md:89:  Keywords: namespaced; canonical UTF-8 NFC bytes; namespace and name separated by a fixed delimiter.\ndocs/design/datom.md:95:  encode(v) = canonical(v)        if |canonical(v)| <= hash-size (32 bytes).\ndocs/design/datom.md:96:  encode(v) = hash(canonical(v))  otherwise.\ndocs/design/datom.md:98:Compound values (maps, lists, records, large blobs) appear as hashes; underlying bytes live in a content-addressed store, fetched lazily.\ndocs/design/datom.md:100:A slot value is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.\ndocs/design/datom.md:104:Every dimension carries content-addressing for free.\ndocs/design/datom.md:107:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\ndocs/design/datom.md:109:Cross-dimension addressing:\ndocs/design/datom.md:115:  Cycles live at the entity layer (in dimensions like d5 where e is a stable local handle): two entities can reference each other via attributes whose v is the other's content hash.\ndocs/design/datom.md:120:  A stream is identified by the hash of its kickoff metadata (creator, schema, dimension, t-zero), not by its content.\ndocs/design/datom.md:128:A datom is the canonical 5-tuple shape [e a v t m] like in Datomic, except the m position is a metadata entity ID. It packages fact + transaction + provenance + entity handle + value in one row, suitable for column-store layouts and EAVT/AEVT indexing. It is the dimension most of dao.jing currently uses.\ndocs/design/datom.md:132:Other dimensions (d1, d3, etc.) are documented separately when introduced.\ndocs/design/datom.md:158:  v: Value. Inline canonical primitive (<= 32 bytes) or 32-byte content hash.\ndocs/design/datom.md:189:  v is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.\ndocs/design/datom.md:301:indexes remain canonical d5.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '1,110p;330,370p;410,490p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '180,220p;400,450p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '300,350p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc | sed -n '519,570p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '1,110p;330,370p;410,490p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '180,220p;400,450p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '300,350p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc | sed -n '519,570p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoJing: Backend-Independent CBOR Storage\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implementation plan; not yet implemented. Architecture-reviewed\n     4\t2026-09-17 (`collab/1789680000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor.*`): no\n     5\tblocking findings, one medium-severity ambiguity (file-backend frame\n     6\townership, corrected below), and one cross-cutting change (the\n     7\t`dao.space.index`/`query` comparator changes) flagged as needing explicit\n     8\tsign-off from `dao.space`'s owners before this plan is built.\n     9\t\n    10\tRelated: [DaoJing](dao.jing.md), [DaoJing DHT](dao.jing.dht.md),\n    11\t[architectural foundations](datom.world.md).\n    12\t\n    13\t## Objective and invariant\n    14\t\n    15\tDaoJing is a backend-independent storage substrate, like Datomic storage.\n    16\tIt can store content in memory, a file, PostgreSQL, S3, or another backend.\n    17\t**This invariant must be preserved. CBOR is the stored data format, not a\n    18\tchoice of storage backend.**\n    19\t\n    20\tReplace Jing's handwritten hash encoding and EDN persistence with canonical\n    21\tCBOR bytes. Jing owns the value-to-bytes contract, content addressing, and\n    22\tintegrity verification. Backends store and retrieve opaque bytes by address\n    23\tand provide their own durability guarantees. They need no knowledge of CBOR,\n    24\tClojure values, datoms, indexes, or manifests. **One exception, narrower\n    25\tthan \"backend\" vs. \"not a backend\":** `dao.jing.file` is the only backend\n    26\tthat re-ingests its own output across process death — it must persist an\n    27\tappend-only log and later replay it with no external message boundary to\n    28\tmark records, so it alone must invent a self-describing frame, which is\n    29\twhy that frame is itself a CBOR structure (a two-element\n    30\t`[digest payload-bytes]` array; see *Memory and files*). `dao.jing.mem`\n    31\tnever persists, so it has no framing at all; `dao.jing.remote`/`dao.jing.dht`\n    32\tget record boundaries for free from Transit's own message envelopes, so\n    33\tthey frame with Base64-inside-Transit, never a raw CBOR array. This is not\n    34\tabout `dao.jing.file` being more or less \"Jing's own\" than any other\n    35\tbackend in this source tree — all of `memory`/`file`/`remote`/`dht` are\n    36\tequally in-repo; only PostgreSQL and S3 are genuinely third-party and not\n    37\tyet built. What is third-party-implementable is the byte-store *contract*\n    38\titself, not any particular shipped implementation of it. The exception is\n    39\tthat `dao.jing.file` parses its own frame with Jing's shared codec, never a\n    40\tdivergent per-backend codec or value interpretation.\n    41\t\n    42\t```text\n    43\twrite: value -> canonical CBOR bytes -> SHA-256 address -> backend\n    44\tread:  address -> backend bytes -> integrity verification -> decoded value\n    45\t\n    46\tbackend: memory | file | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT | ...\n    47\t```\n    48\t\n    49\tPostgreSQL and S3 illustrate the required interchangeability; implementing\n    50\tnew PostgreSQL or S3 backends is outside this codec migration.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Community precedent and integration choice\n    53\t\n    54\tReuse [Boring](https://github.com/replikativ/boring), the CBOR serializer built\n    55\tfor Datahike. Konserve integrates it as a pluggable JVM/JavaScript serializer\n    56\t(id 3), but marks it BETA, \"Not yet recommended for production stores\";\n    57\tFressian remains its default (verified against\n    58\t[Konserve serializers](https://github.com/replikativ/konserve/blob/main/src/konserve/serializers.cljc),\n    59\t2026-09-16).\n    60\tJing will reuse serialization directly without adopting Konserve's storage\n    61\tAPI, mutable document operations, or Datahike-specific type handlers.\n    62\t\n    63\tPin `org.replikativ/boring` to `0.1.30` on JVM and JavaScript, verified as the\n    64\tlatest release on 2026-09-16 against\n    65\t[Clojars](https://clojars.org/org.replikativ/boring). Implement the same\n    66\tJing-supported representation on Dart. Boring's upstream\n    67\t[compatibility policy](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/doc/COMPATIBILITY.md)\n    68\tpreserves released tag meanings and older-output readability, but does not\n    69\tpromise identical encoding bytes across releases (verified against that\n    70\tpolicy, 2026-09-16). Jing's pin and frozen byte/hash fixtures own byte\n    71\tstability; Konserve's integration does not. Dependency upgrades must pass\n    72\tthose fixtures without regeneration.\n    73\t\n    74\tSelect `:canonical` and explicitly set encode options for stringref off,\n    75\tshapes off, and no index frame. Verified against the\n    76\t[Boring README](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/README.md),\n    77\t2026-09-16: `:clojure` defaults to string deduplication and preserved float\n    78\twidth, `:canonical` uses bytewise key ordering and shortest numeric forms,\n    79\tand `:canonical-rfc7049` uses length-first ordering. Option precedence over\n    80\tprofile defaults is unverified; fixtures must prove the effective options,\n    81\trather than relying on profile selection alone.\n    82\t\n    83\t## Layering and interfaces\n    84\t\n    85\t- Introduce `dao.jing.cbor/encode` and `decode`, operating on host byte\n    86\t  arrays. Share normalization and validation across hosts; isolate Dart's\n    87\t  codec implementation behind the same interface.\n    88\t- Preserve the public value-facing `materialize!`, `get`, `segment-key`,\n    89\t  `content-hash`, and `close!` operations. Existing callers still write and\n    90\t  read values.\n    91\t- Introduce an explicit byte-store boundary below those operations:\n    92\t  `{:put-bytes-fn f :get-bytes-fn g :close-fn c}`. Put takes an address and\n    93\t  canonical payload bytes and returns `:inserted` or `:present`; get takes\n    94\t  an address and caller-supplied not-found sentinel and returns bytes or\n    95\t  that sentinel. A shared Jing wrapper exposes the existing content-store\n    96\t  handle over this byte store.\n    97\t- This contract is effect-payload-shaped (address, bytes → verdict), so it\n    98\t  can become the deferred effect-stream write path in `dao.jing.md` without\n    99\t  a contract break; the synchronous function handle is its present-day\n   100\t  shape, not a commitment. The put half is effect-shaped as stated; the get\n   101\t  half becomes effect-shaped once its outcome is the explicit\n   102\t  `{:found? boolean, :bytes b}` envelope — `dao.jing.remote` already\n   103\t  answers the `{:found? boolean, :value v}` shape this generalizes\n   104\t  (`remote.cljc:55-60,84-85`; `:bytes` is this plan's future key, not\n   105\t  `:value`, since the value here is a byte representation, not a decoded\n   106\t  Clojure value) — with bytes in a portable byte representation — the\n   107\t  caller-supplied not-found sentinel is a wrapper-level convenience over\n   108\t  that envelope, not part of the contract's streamable shape.\n   109\t- Encode once before a write, derive the address from those bytes, and pass\n   110\t  those same bytes to the backend. On `:present`, read back and verify the\n   330\t  extending VM arithmetic is outside this migration.\n   331\t\n   332\t## Backend changes\n   333\t\n   334\tAll existing backends move to the shared byte-store boundary. No backend\n   335\tselects a different value codec or re-hashes a host-specific representation\n   336\t— with one named exception, `dao.jing.file`'s own frame parsing (see the\n   337\tObjective's carve-out, and *Memory and files* below).\n   338\t\n   339\t### Memory and files\n   340\t\n   341\tThe memory backend stores byte snapshots under content addresses. The file\n   342\tbackend retains its append-only length framing, durability rules, duplicate\n   343\tvalidation, and torn-tail recovery. Each frame contains a CBOR\n   344\t`[digest payload-bytes]` record: digest is a raw 32-byte SHA-256 byte string\n   345\tand payload-bytes contains the exact canonical payload. **`dao.jing.file`\n   346\titself owns parsing and constructing this two-element frame**, with Jing's\n   347\town shared codec — the exception the Objective names above, needed because\n   348\t`dao.jing.file`'s frame shape is itself a CBOR structure, unlike\n   349\t`dao.jing.mem`'s unframed map or `dao.jing.remote`/`dao.jing.dht`'s\n   350\tBase64-inside-Transit framing. The wrapper owns keyword↔digest\n   351\tconversion, so file framing has no identifier-codec\n   352\tdependency. Replay checks the digest length and compares it byte-for-byte\n   353\twith the payload's SHA-256. The outer record is backend framing and does\n   354\tnot contribute to the content hash. It is exactly one definite two-element\n   355\tCBOR array of byte strings, with shortest lengths and no trailing data,\n   356\tinside the existing four-byte big-endian signed length prefix; reject a\n   357\tframe exceeding that prefix's positive range before append.\n   358\t\n   359\tThe byte-string payload is intentional: a file backend delegates payload\n   360\tcanonicality acceptance at replay to the shared Jing validator, then stores\n   361\tand serves the original opaque bytes. It never replaces them with a decoded\n   362\tand re-encoded value. Duplicate digests require identical payload bytes.\n   363\tPostgreSQL byte columns and S3 objects obey the same ingress rule when opened\n   364\tover externally supplied bytes.\n   365\t\n   366\t### Remote and DHT\n   367\t\n   368\tCarry canonical payload bytes as padded standard-alphabet Base64 strings\n   369\t(no whitespace or URL-safe alphabet) inside the existing Transit\n   370\toperation envelopes. Encode and decode this transport representation at\n   410\tcontract requires that an existing address is never silently overwritten\n   411\twith different bytes. Neither backend needs CBOR value handlers.\n   412\t\n   413\t## Addressing and clean break\n   414\t\n   415\tHash canonical payload bytes directly with SHA-256. Preserve the address\n   416\tshape `:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>`. Keep the existing string-based\n   417\t`sha256` helper for its other callers and add a separate byte-hashing function.\n   418\t\n   419\tEvery newly encoded value receives its CBOR-derived address. No legacy\n   420\treader, old-address alias, or graph migration is included. Reject old\n   421\tcomplete EDN file records without rewriting them. Existing stores and\n   422\tpublished references must be rebuilt together, including address-bearing\n   423\tindexes, ASTs, and continuations. **This states the rebuild's precondition\n   424\texplicitly: with no legacy reader, the only reconstruction path is\n   425\treplaying original values from their intake streams, not reading them back\n   426\tout of a rejected old store.** A value whose intake stream has since\n   427\tevicted it (a `:dao.stream/gap`) and whose old store is rejected under this\n   428\tplan has no remaining source — that content is unrecoverable, not merely\n   429\tinconvenient to rebuild. Rebuild readiness should be checked against\n   430\tretained intake history before this migration lands, not assumed. Existing\n   431\ttorn-tail recovery applies only\n   432\tafter the file is recognized as a valid new-format log; an old-format file\n   433\tmust not be treated as an empty or recoverable new store.\n   434\tAn empty file is a new log. Recognize a nonempty log only after its first\n   435\tcomplete frame passes the new frame, digest, and payload checks. Validate\n   436\tall complete frames before truncating an incomplete tail; any corrupt\n   437\tcomplete frame or a nonempty file with no valid first frame fails without\n   438\tmutation. This deliberately refuses an interrupted first write that cannot\n   439\tbe distinguished from foreign content without adding a format header. The\n   440\tremedy for an interrupted first write is to delete or recreate the file: the\n   441\tinterrupted put was never acknowledged, so no acknowledged content is lost\n   442\tand recreation is always safe.\n   443\t\n   444\tUpdate the main Jing design, backend documentation, and downstream canonical\n   445\tencoding references to describe the shared byte-store boundary and remove\n   446\tthe superseded transitional-printer limitations. Name exactly which\n   447\t`dao.jing.md` open items this plan retires — the canonical-encoding item\n   448\twith its three residuals (symbol metadata not address-significant,\n   449\tpathological print collisions, ambient print-var leakage), byte-array\n   450\tidentity hashing, and metadata carriage for the file/remote/DHT codecs —\n   451\tand which stay open: the intake transport's portable domain still carries\n   452\tno metadata, byte strings, or rich numerics (`dao.stream` Transit), so\n   453\tsuch payloads reach Jing only through direct `materialize!` calls and the\n   454\tintake fail-closed rule carries forward unchanged. The ClojureDart `list`\n   455\tproducer obligation likewise carries forward, as *Encoding contract*\n   456\trecords.\n   457\t\n   458\t## Implementation sequence and validation\n   459\t\n   460\t1. Write encoding-contract tests and frozen CBOR hex/SHA-256 fixtures before\n   461\t   implementation. Pin Boring and implement the JVM/JavaScript codec wrapper,\n   462\t   normalization, named extensions, and numeric constructors.\n   463\t2. Implement the matching Dart codec and carriers, either hand-rolled or\n   464\t   over a Dart CBOR package, behind the shared interface — frozen fixtures\n   465\t   own conformance either way. Require all three hosts\n   466\t   to read each other's fixtures and reproduce identical canonical bytes.\n   467\t3. Add byte hashing and the common value-facing wrapper; migrate memory and\n   468\t   file backends to opaque bytes. Preserve public value-facing call sites.\n   469\t   `dao.jing.cbor` exports the portable `=`/`hash`/`compare`; the numeric\n   470\t   arm of `dao.space.index/compare-vals` (the EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET datom\n   471\t   comparators) and `dao.space.query`'s comparison builtins route through\n   472\t   them, with the arithmetic builtins left host-native per *Numeric\n   473\t   identity* — the deepest of this migration's changes outside the\n   474\t   `dao.jing*` namespaces, threading new runtime behavior into an existing\n   475\t   consumer's comparison/equality logic (step 5's `dao.data.btree.md` §5.2\n   476\t   change is shallower: a pre-authorized default flip, not new code or a\n   477\t   new runtime dependency).\n   478\t4. Migrate remote and DHT byte transport, including validation, missing-value\n   479\t   behavior, transport limits, and errors.\n   480\t5. Update documentation and downstream address fixtures — including\n   481\t   `dao.data.btree.md` §5.2, whose same-host-only verification default is\n   482\t   **a default flip, not a code or format change**: §5.2 already\n   483\t   pre-authorizes this exact trigger (\"when the canonical byte encoding\n   484\t   lands... the default flips to on and the same-host restriction\n   485\t   disappears — the check itself needs no format change, only a stable\n   486\t   encoding under it\"). This CBOR migration is that stable encoding\n   487\t   landing; `dao.data.btree.storage`'s rehash-and-compare check is\n   488\t   unchanged code, now verified safe to default on. Then run the\n   489\t   repository's JVM, Node, and Dart suites and applicable lint checks.\n   490\t\n   180\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   181\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   182\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   183\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   184\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   185\t\n   186\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   187\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   188\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   189\t\n   190\tThe target encoding is a canonical flat byte representation suitable for\n   191\tcross-platform hashing and in-place reading. The current implementation uses\n   192\tan order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer (`dao.jing/order-normalize`\n   193\tand `canonical-print`) as a transitional encoder — deterministic and\n   194\torder-insensitive, but not yet the pinned canonical byte encoding. This is the\n   195\tfirst open item under *Open items and current limitations*.\n   196\t\n   197\tThe transitional encoder's current contract, precisely: collection metadata\n   198\t(on maps, sets, vectors, lists, and seqs) is address-significant, except\n   199\treader-position keys (`:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, `:end-column`), which\n   200\tare stripped before hashing, and empty metadata, which is dropped rather than\n   201\ttreated as distinct from no metadata. Scalar metadata (on symbols — no\n   202\tportable host lets a keyword carry metadata) is not address-significant\n   203\t— it is silently ignored, a known\n   204\tresidual pending the pinned canonical byte encoding. Lists and seqs of equal\n   205\tcontent share one address (`=` calls them equal and both print the same way);\n   206\tvectors, sets, and maps are each their own type and never collide with\n   207\tanother, for any non-pathological scalar (see the pathological-symbol\n   208\tresidual under *Open items and current limitations*). Records are not a\n   209\tsupported payload: `content-hash` throws rather\n   210\tthan silently addressing a record as its equal plain map, since the\n   211\tparticipating hosts cannot agree on how to print one. `materialize!`'s\n   212\t`:present` read-back is verified by re-hashing the stored value and comparing\n   213\tit to the claimed address, not by `=`, since `=` ignores metadata and a\n   214\tmetadata-only mismatch is a real collision.\n   215\t\n   216\t## Storage ignorance\n   217\t\n   218\tDaoJing assigns identity to opaque content but does not interpret the data\n   219\tstructure stored at that identity. In particular, it does not:\n   220\t\n   400\t**The observer is implemented.** `observer-state` and `observe-step!` provide\n   401\tthe explicit intake-pool walk described in *Cursor tracking and recovery*,\n   402\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   403\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Content addressing is implemented, transitionally.** `content-hash` hashes\n   406\tthe order-normalized print of a value; `segment-key` mints\n   407\t`:segment/sha256-<hash>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict address\n   408\ttest the backend layer enforces. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*, the\n   409\tencoder is transitional until the pinned canonical byte encoding lands.\n   410\t\n   411\t## Open items and current limitations\n   412\t\n   413\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized hand-printer encoder must be\n   414\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   415\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   416\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   417\t  and every minted address change together. Three residuals of the\n   418\t  transitional encoder are deferred to that landing: scalar (symbol)\n   419\t  metadata is not address-significant; pathological symbols whose print text\n   420\t  mimics another value's print (e.g. `(symbol \"42\")` vs `42`) can collide;\n   421\t  and ambient print-var bindings (`*print-readably*` and similar) still\n   422\t  reach scalar bytes, since `canonical-print` delegates scalars to `pr-str`\n   423\t  — collection structure and order are rendered by `canonical-print`\n   424\t  itself, so only scalar leaves reach the host printer.\n   425\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   426\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   427\t  (`{:line … :column … :end-line … :end-column … :tag PersistentList}`).\n   428\t  `order-normalize`, `yin.vm`'s semantic-bytecode projection, and the\n   429\t  ClojureDart Transit decoders (`dao.stream.transit.cljd` behind\n   430\t  `dao.stream.ws`'s incoming frames, and the older `dao.stream.transit`)\n   431\t  clear metadata on the lists they mint, so neither normalization nor a list\n   432\t  decoded off the wire fabricates it. Any other Dart code that builds a\n   433\t  payload with `list` still hands `dao.jing` that metadata, and its `:tag`\n   434\t  survives the reader-position strip, so the payload addresses differently\n   435\t  from the equal list built on another host. Each such constructor must clear\n   436\t  it the same way (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`) until the ClojureDart\n   437\t  defect is fixed upstream or the pinned canonical encoding decides the fate\n   438\t  of metadata.\n   439\t- **Byte arrays are hashed by identity, not content.** `dao.jing.md` lists\n   440\t  byte arrays as a supported representation-level type, but the transitional\n   441\t  encoder's scalar branch falls through to `pr-str`, which on the JVM prints\n   442\t  a byte array as an identity-bearing object literal (`#object[\"[B\" 0x...\n   443\t  \"...\"]`); other hosts print their own identity-bearing form. Two\n   444\t  content-equal byte arrays currently mint different addresses. No\n   445\t  current producer emits byte-array payloads, so this is latent; it must be\n   446\t  fixed (a proper byte-array print rule, or promotion into the pinned\n   447\t  canonical byte encoding) before any producer relies on byte-array content\n   448\t  addressing.\n   449\t- **Backends and transports must fail closed on metadata they cannot carry.**\n   450\t  Metadata is now address-significant in the transitional encoder, but no\n   300\t     :cljd (let [padded (pad-message bs)\n   301\t                 chunks (partition 64 padded)\n   302\t                 final-h (reduce process-chunk initial-h chunks)]\n   303\t             (bytes->hex final-h))))\n   304\t\n   305\t\n   306\t(defn content-hash\n   307\t  \"SHA-256 of the order-normalized print of v. Total over any value.\n   308\t\n   309\t   Transitional: order-normalized pr-str is the current encoder, NOT the\n   310\t   pinned cross-platform canonical byte encoding the spec calls for\n   311\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical encoding), which is still an open\n   312\t   migration item. Content addresses are portable only between\n   313\t   implementations sharing this exact print rule; when the canonical\n   314\t   encoding lands, content-hash, segment-key, and every minted address\n   315\t   change together.\"\n   316\t  [v]\n   317\t  (sha256 (order-normalized-print v)))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn canonical-bytes\n   321\t  \"Host UTF-8 bytes (byte[] / Uint8Array / Uint8List) of the\n   322\t   order-normalized print of v — the exact bytes `content-hash` digests, so\n   323\t   `(sha256-bytes (canonical-bytes v))` is `(content-hash v)`. This is the\n   324\t   byte form Jing content travels in across a `dao.stream` boundary: the\n   325\t   Jing boundary adapter (`dao.jing.stream`) wraps these per codec profile,\n   326\t   so a payload crosses any transport as its addressed bytes and no\n   327\t   transport's value domain ever re-encodes — or silently normalizes — the\n   328\t   content itself.\"\n   329\t  [v]\n   330\t  (let [text (order-normalized-print v)]\n   331\t    #?(:clj (.getBytes ^String text \"UTF-8\")\n   332\t       :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (crypt/stringToUtf8ByteArray text))\n   333\t       :cljd (utf8-bytes text))))\n   334\t\n   335\t\n   336\t(defn segment-key\n   337\t  \"Mint the content-addressed key for an opaque payload, derived solely from\n   338\t   the payload: :segment/sha256-<hash(payload)>. The algorithm prefix is\n   339\t   load-bearing twice over: it names the hash function, and it keeps the\n   340\t   keyword readable EDN — a name starting with a bare hex digit cannot\n   341\t   survive print -> read, which poisons every EDN boundary content\n   342\t   addresses cross.\"\n   343\t  [v]\n   344\t  (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" (content-hash v))))\n   345\t\n   346\t\n   347\t(defn segment-address?\n   348\t  \"True when x is a content address of the form :segment/sha256-<64 hex>.\n   349\t   This is the only address class DaoJing reads or writes: arbitrary keys\n   350\t   and mutable roots are outside DaoJing.\"\n   519\t(deftest values-the-file-codec-cannot-carry-are-refused-not-corrupted\n   520\t  ;; one law on every host: a durable round trip is exact, or it is refused\n   521\t  ;; loudly — at write, nothing stored (Dart's list case), or at read as\n   522\t  ;; :hash-mismatch (CLJS's -0.0 case) — never a silent change of value.\n   523\t  ;; No case requires a refusal on any host, so a codec fix cannot break\n   524\t  ;; it; :exact-on names only the hosts already shown to carry the value.\n   525\t  (doseq [{:keys [label ast exact-on]}\n   526\t          [{:label \"negative zero\",\n   527\t            :ast (app (v 'list) (lit -0.0) (lit 0.0)),\n   528\t            :exact-on #{:clj :cljd}}\n   529\t           {:label \"a list of integers\",\n   530\t            :ast (app (v 'list) (lit (list 1 2))),\n   531\t            :exact-on #{:clj}}\n   532\t           {:label \"a list holding a double\",\n   533\t            :ast (app (v 'list) (lit (list 1 2.5))),\n   534\t            :exact-on #{:clj}}]]\n   535\t    (testing label\n   536\t      (let [path (temp-path)]\n   537\t        (try\n   538\t          (let [store (jing-file/create-content-file path)\n   539\t                projected (:projected (persist! ast (recording-writer) store))\n   540\t                _ (jing/close! store)\n   541\t                original (select-keys (debruijn/project-datoms\n   542\t                                        (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)))\n   543\t                                      [:fingerprint :records])]\n   544\t            (if (= :ok (:outcome projected))\n   545\t              (let [reopened (jing-file/create-content-file path)\n   546\t                    stored (:yin.debruijn/datoms\n   547\t                             (jing/get reopened (:address projected) ::absent))\n   548\t                    _ (jing/close! reopened)\n   549\t                    read-back (try (debruijn/datoms->projected stored)\n   550\t                                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n   551\t                                     (ex-data e)))]\n   552\t                (is (or (= original read-back) (= :hash-mismatch (:rule read-back)))\n   553\t                    \"stored: exact, or refused at read as :hash-mismatch\"))\n   554\t              (do (is (= :diagnostic (:outcome projected)))\n   555\t                  (is (= :projected-write-failed (get-in projected [:diagnostic :rule]))\n   556\t                      \"refused at write by the store's own codec check\")\n   557\t                  (is (empty? (jing-file/records path))\n   558\t                      \"nothing was half-written for the refused program\")))\n   559\t            (when (contains? exact-on host)\n   560\t              (is (= :ok (:outcome projected)))\n   561\t              (when (= :ok (:outcome projected))\n   562\t                (is (= original\n   563\t                       (let [reopened (jing-file/create-content-file path)\n   564\t                             stored (:yin.debruijn/datoms\n   565\t                                      (jing/get reopened (:address projected) ::absent))]\n   566\t                         (jing/close! reopened)\n   567\t                         (debruijn/datoms->projected stored)))\n   568\t                    \"hosts already shown to carry the value carry it exactly\"))))\n   569\t          (finally (cleanup-file path)))))))\n   570\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.md | sed -n '50,78p;100,140p'; rg -n \\\"dimension|descriptor\\\" src/cljc test docs/design | grep -v debruijn | head -120\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.md | sed -n '50,78p;100,140p'; rg -n \\\"dimension|descriptor\\\" src/cljc test docs/design | grep -v debruijn | head -120\"","aggregated_output":"    50\t\n    51\t# META-PROTOCOL\n    52\t\n    53\tThe meta-protocol is the only universal piece. The vocabulary of dimensions is open.\n    54\t\n    55\tA dimension dn is declared as a small d3 subgraph:\n    56\t  :dim/arity         n (the slot count).\n    57\t  :dim/slots         ordered list of (slot-name, slot-type, slot-role).\n    58\t  :dim/encoding      canonical-encoding rule (parameterized by slot types).\n    59\t  :dim/projection-to declared morphism dn -> dm (m < n).\n    60\t  :dim/lift-from     declared morphism dm -> dn (with default-supplying function or pointer).\n    61\t\n    62\tThe content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity.\n    63\tNew dimensions are introduced by publishing such a bundle. No external schema authority is needed.\n    64\t\n    65\tPer-tuple hash:\n    66\t  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\n    67\t  Different dimensions never collide on the same slot values.\n    68\t  A d3 fact and a d5 fact (datom) with the same (s, a, v) hash to different values: they are different things in the moduli space.\n    69\t\n    70\tMorphisms:\n    71\t  Projections (dn -> dm where m < n): drop slots; lossy.\n    72\t  Lifts (dm -> dn where n > m): add slots, with defaults from the ingesting segment.\n    73\t  Each new dn publishes morphisms to/from anchor dimensions (typically d3 for fact-shaped, d1 universally).\n    74\t  Composite morphisms are computed transitively.\n    75\t\n    76\tEquivalence:\n    77\t  Two tuples are equal iff their canonical hashes match within the same dimension.\n    78\t  Cross-dimensional equality goes through projection: a in dn matches b in dm iff pi(a) = b.\n   100\tA slot value is always small: an inline canonical primitive or a fixed-size content hash.\n   101\t\n   102\t# CONTENT ADDRESSING\n   103\t\n   104\tEvery dimension carries content-addressing for free.\n   105\t\n   106\tPer-tuple hash (universal):\n   107\t  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\n   108\t\n   109\tCross-dimension addressing:\n   110\t  d1 floor: every tuple reduces to its hash, a d1 tuple. Universal.\n   111\t  d3 floor: every fact-shaped tuple projects to (s, a, v). Universal for the fact-shaped subset.\n   112\t\n   113\tCycles:\n   114\t  Pure content addressing forbids value-level cycles (the hash would have to be known to compute itself).\n   115\t  Cycles live at the entity layer (in dimensions like d5 where e is a stable local handle): two entities can reference each other via attributes whose v is the other's content hash.\n   116\t  Values stay acyclic. Entities can cycle.\n   117\t\n   118\tStreams:\n   119\t  Streams are temporal and unbounded; their content cannot be hashed in finite time.\n   120\t  A stream is identified by the hash of its kickoff metadata (creator, schema, dimension, t-zero), not by its content.\n   121\t  Values are atemporal. Streams are temporal. They are different kinds of thing.\n   122\t\n   123\tHashing is triggered by persistence, not by existence.\n   124\tLike git: working directory has no SHA; only committed content is hashed.\n   125\t\n   126\t# d5: DATOMS (PROVENANCED TEMPORAL FACTS)\n   127\t\n   128\tA datom is the canonical 5-tuple shape [e a v t m] like in Datomic, except the m position is a metadata entity ID. It packages fact + transaction + provenance + entity handle + value in one row, suitable for column-store layouts and EAVT/AEVT indexing. It is the dimension most of dao.jing currently uses.\n   129\t\n   130\tThe remainder of this document defines the datom (d5) in detail: components,\n   131\tsizing, value constraints, reserved entities, and source scope.\n   132\tOther dimensions (d1, d3, etc.) are documented separately when introduced.\n   133\t\n   134\tTuple shape: `[e a v t m]`.\n   135\t\n   136\tEntity ids are stream-local. A query interpreter retains the physical source\n   137\tas context and does not flatten independent histories into one relation.\n   138\t\n   139\tComponents:\n   140\t  e: Entity ID. Local handle for evolving identity. Relative offset from zero basis.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:9:A WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:15:honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:21:that ring buffer; the endpoint produces a descriptor carrying its address and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:24:the host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:30:stream's descriptor.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:34:A WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:40:`descriptor` with reachability for the endpoint through which they attached\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:42:Two endpoints may therefore produce different descriptors for the same served\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:44:`:dao.stream/attachment` distinguishes connections; neither the descriptor nor\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:58:| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:59:| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:98:requires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:448:key is contract-owned. The exact key set is settled by the descriptor gate;\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:451:- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:461:- The descriptor carries reachability and logical-stream identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:467:Attaching presents the descriptor's canonical lookup path, and the endpoint\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:481:descriptor. Resolution is an exact string lookup on this canonical form.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:536:  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:537:  a descriptor it cannot read, `:dao.stream/transport-error` for a handler\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:553:  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:582:  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:591:  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:592:  descriptor rejoins the same stream. What a client must not confuse with it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:600:descriptor can.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:690:- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:560:arity `(attach attach! descriptor {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g})` returns\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:854:| T3 | The kept-cursor `attach` arity must say how `:ingress-gaps` is seeded (glm P3) | §4.2 names the arity `(attach attach! descriptor {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g})` |\nsrc/cljc/dao/postgraphics/raster.cljc:140:  \"Given base RGBA bytes (0–255 values) and dimensions, returns a vector of\nsrc/cljc/dao/postgraphics/raster.cljc:143:   previous, halving each dimension (floored, min 1) until 1×1.\"\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:21:- `docs/design/datom.md` — datom moduli space, dimensions, gauge/content-hash framing\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:29:**A DaoSpace is a catalog of tuples that enter via dao.stream member logs and are read by interpreters — a coordination medium whose contents are that catalog.** Structurally it is the accreting, content-addressed catalog of every tuple written into it; functionally it is the medium through which resources and interpreters coordinate. The two are one thing: agents coordinate *by* writing and querying the catalog, and its currency is the tuple (a datom). A **resource** is anything expressible as datoms — data, a dao.stream (its descriptor is a datom), yin.vm bytecode (AST datoms), schema, provenance, an agent's belief state — so the catalog of tuples is implicitly a catalog of resources. **Interpreters** read those tuples and realize them into meaning or behavior.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:33:This is datom.world's modern tuple space, generalized: not only streams-and-readers but *any resource* and interpreters, because everything reduces to tuples (CLAUDE.md: \"everything is data, code is data, runtime state is data\"; `datom.md`: datoms are the universal format for DaoDB, AST, schema, provenance). The tuples are **named, n-dimensional** datoms (d1/d3/d5/d10), not Linda's positional, untyped arrays — so a tuple carries its own meaning, and coordination never depends on agents agreeing on field order. Unlike traditional message passing (explicit sender → receiver), DaoSpace enables **stigmergic coordination**: producers leave tuples (traces), consumers react to them, decoupled in time and identity. The space is a *passive* medium — it does not orchestrate, schedule, or decide, and it does not itself coordinate; it *enables* coordination by being the shared tuple substrate. (The medium is a logical rendezvous, which may itself be distributed; it provides a shared catalog, not global agreement — consensus, where needed, is layered on top.)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:35:Membership is **dynamic**: opening a member log (`ds/open!` through the handle) *is* joining the space; closing it leaves. Every writer owns its **own** log, so writes never contend or need routing — there is no shared write cursor to merge. The space is heterogeneous along two axes: member logs differ in **dimension/type** (one carries only d5, another only d10), and in how strict they are (some enforce their type on write, some accept anything). Reads range over all member logs at once; an **interpreter** is the read-side projection that folds those logs into a queryable view — its materialization of a stream is a **slice**: the stream quotiented by an interpreter-defined equivalence relation.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:58:any coherent bundle of those tuples — data, a dao.stream descriptor, yin.vm AST,\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:73:stream is typed by dimension (the tuple's shape) and optionally slot types, and\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:76:n-dimensional** datoms (d1/d3/d5/d10), so a tuple carries its own meaning —\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:80:- **Dimension/type** — a given stream is homogeneous in dimension (all d5, or all\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:82:  may carry different dimensions.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:160:│ ds/append! frames → │  │ ds/append! frames → │           typed by dimension, strict?)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:188:  a dao.stream descriptor, a yin.vm AST, a schema, a provenance record, an agent's\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:195:- **A dao.stream is a resource.** Its descriptor is plain data, hence a datom, so a\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:198:  through streams\"). An interpreter *realizes* a stream-descriptor resource by\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:202:  ;; A stream descriptor is a datom-resource; realizing it = open!\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:203:  ;; (illustrative — resource->descriptor / read-resource are not yet implemented;\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:205:  (ds/open! (resource->descriptor (read-resource space stream-eid)))\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:218:**self-contained** resources (an AST, a schema, a dao.stream descriptor), whose\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:229:Each member log declares its **dimension** and, optionally, its **slot types**.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:231:the hash of `(creator, schema, dimension, t-zero)` (see\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:232:`docs/design/datom.md`, CONTENT ADDRESSING > Streams), so dimension and\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:236:A stream is **homogeneous in dimension**: a given stream carries only d5, or only\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:242:{:dimension :d5\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:246:Conformance is a **per-stream policy**, carried in the stream descriptor much like\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:249:- `true` — the stream validates each datom against its declared dimension and\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:260:                       :dimension :d5 :slots {...} :strict? true}))\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:264:                       :dimension :d5 :strict? false}))\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:290:- **d1 floor** — equal iff same content hash `hash(dimension-hash ‖ slots)`. The\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:291:  finest, dimension-aware identity.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:297:projection to a coarser dimension, and so on. Each relation slices the same\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:317:dimension-homogeneous but the space is heterogeneous, cross-log joins happen on\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:396:dimension n\" (`datom.md` line 7), with content hash as gauge-invariant\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:397:identity and entity IDs as local coordinates. Here \"dimension n\" is that moduli\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:398:grading (arity + encoding + morphisms), not a vector-space or manifold dimension.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:456:dimension). These are different quotients of one total space, coexisting via\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:865:(defn open! [name-or-descriptor] ...)               ; => DaoSpace handle\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:870:(ds/defopen :dao-stream [{:keys [space name dimension slots strict?]}] ...)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1102:declares a dimension and slot types and rejects non-conforming datoms on `put!`;\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1340:- `:strict? true` path: validate/coerce dimension, slot types, cardinality,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ffi.md:117:The `create-vm` call can also take explicit `:call-in` and `:call-out` stream descriptors to override the default in-memory ring buffers.\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:224:descriptor (see `dao.space.transactor.md`, and `dao.space.md`, *The Write\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:104:Callers explicitly wrap arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuples with\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:137:arbitrary-dimensional exact positional matching/unification (with explicit `&`\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:349:- **Positive-conjunction pattern clauses** — arbitrary mixed-dimensional\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:477:  append-only, retained sequence *over time*, so a descriptor can name it\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:486:  `:dao.stream/relation` descriptor carried its tuples *inside itself* and\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:487:  \"opened\" into a record that read them back by index. A descriptor names a\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:488:  stream; a descriptor that *is* the data is not reachability data, it is a\ndocs/design/datom.md:2:description: Moduli space of tuples; meta-protocol over open dimensions; N-dimensional tuples, entity IDs, content addressing, namespaces; datom defined as [e a v t m]; source scope is interpreter context, never a tuple slot\ndocs/design/datom.md:7:Tuples are elements in a moduli space, graded by dimension n.\ndocs/design/datom.md:8:A tuple can be any dimension/size; each dimension dn is a distinct kind of fact, fit for a distinct role.\ndocs/design/datom.md:10:The moduli space is open: applications declare new dimensions as needed. No dimension is canonical.\ndocs/design/datom.md:20:  d3: (s, a, v). Bare facts (RDF-style triples). The semantic floor for fact-shaped dimensions.\ndocs/design/datom.md:25:  d1 (content-addressing floor): every tuple of any dimension reduces to its hash, itself a d1 tuple. Universal addressability.\ndocs/design/datom.md:26:  d3 (semantic floor): every fact-shaped dimension projects to (subject, attribute, value). Universal interpretability for the fact-shaped subset.\ndocs/design/datom.md:28:Not every dimension is fact-shaped. Some are tuple-shaped without subject (capabilities, signatures, sensor frames).\ndocs/design/datom.md:31:Universal principles (apply to all dimensions):\ndocs/design/datom.md:36:  Restrictions are a feature: dimension choice constrains shape, enabling efficient per-shape indexing.\ndocs/design/datom.md:40:  Each dimension is a different chart on that universe.\ndocs/design/datom.md:41:  A tuple at dimension n is a tuple-shaped event in n coordinates.\ndocs/design/datom.md:45:  Interpreters observe parts of the stream and construct higher-dimensional structures.\ndocs/design/datom.md:47:  Higher-dimensional tuples can serve specialized streams, for example\ndocs/design/datom.md:53:The meta-protocol is the only universal piece. The vocabulary of dimensions is open.\ndocs/design/datom.md:55:A dimension dn is declared as a small d3 subgraph:\ndocs/design/datom.md:62:The content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity.\ndocs/design/datom.md:63:New dimensions are introduced by publishing such a bundle. No external schema authority is needed.\ndocs/design/datom.md:66:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\ndocs/design/datom.md:67:  Different dimensions never collide on the same slot values.\ndocs/design/datom.md:73:  Each new dn publishes morphisms to/from anchor dimensions (typically d3 for fact-shaped, d1 universally).\ndocs/design/datom.md:77:  Two tuples are equal iff their canonical hashes match within the same dimension.\ndocs/design/datom.md:78:  Cross-dimensional equality goes through projection: a in dn matches b in dm iff pi(a) = b.\ndocs/design/datom.md:104:Every dimension carries content-addressing for free.\ndocs/design/datom.md:107:  hash(tuple) = hash(dimension-hash || canonical-encoded-slots-in-order).\ndocs/design/datom.md:109:Cross-dimension addressing:\ndocs/design/datom.md:115:  Cycles live at the entity layer (in dimensions like d5 where e is a stable local handle): two entities can reference each other via attributes whose v is the other's content hash.\ndocs/design/datom.md:120:  A stream is identified by the hash of its kickoff metadata (creator, schema, dimension, t-zero), not by its content.\ndocs/design/datom.md:128:A datom is the canonical 5-tuple shape [e a v t m] like in Datomic, except the m position is a metadata entity ID. It packages fact + transaction + provenance + entity handle + value in one row, suitable for column-store layouts and EAVT/AEVT indexing. It is the dimension most of dao.jing currently uses.\ndocs/design/datom.md:132:Other dimensions (d1, d3, etc.) are documented separately when introduced.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.v2.divergence-register.md:130:| `:stream/make` | `ds/open!` with a hardcoded `:ringbuffer` descriptor | the host's `:make-stream`; `invalid-spec`, `not-found`, `transport-error` fail construction |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.v2.divergence-register.md:241:attaches it through a unary capability and a portable descriptor, and drives\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:42:   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:43:4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:48:Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:58:| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:59:| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:91:descriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:104:  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:208:  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:209:  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:211:  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:212:  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:213:  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
